00:00:15.650 --> 00:00:18.640
- [Narrator] A coronary
angioplasty procedure is also known

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.263
as percutaneous coronary intervention.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:24.360
The procedure is done on blood vessels

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:26.193
called coronary arteries.

00:00:27.170 --> 00:00:29.543
They supply your heart muscle with oxygen.

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:35.970
The goal is to restore
blood flow if a substance

00:00:35.970 --> 00:00:39.463
called plaque has significantly
narrowed these vessels.

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.490
To begin the procedure,

00:00:42.490 --> 00:00:44.853
the doctor will numb
the skin in your groin.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:49.410
A needle will be placed through your skin

00:00:49.410 --> 00:00:51.023
and into the femoral artery.

00:00:52.270 --> 00:00:54.790
Next, a flexible guide wire will be passed

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:57.190
through the needle into your artery.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:00:59.283
Then, the needle will be withdrawn.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:01.760
It will be exchanged

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:05.270
for a small, flexible
tube called a sheath.

00:01:05.270 --> 00:01:07.463
This permits access into your artery.

00:01:08.380 --> 00:01:11.530
You may feel pressure when
the doctor inserts the sheath,

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:14.453
but you will not feel it
moving inside your artery.

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.303
Next, the guide wire will be
advanced up to your heart.

00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:24.170
A flexible tube called a
catheter will be advanced

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:26.563
over the wire to your coronary arteries.

00:01:28.570 --> 00:01:30.580
The progress of the
procedure will be checked

00:01:30.580 --> 00:01:33.730
with an X-ray device called a fluoroscope.

00:01:33.730 --> 00:01:36.653
At this point, your doctor
will remove the guide wire.

00:01:37.510 --> 00:01:40.320
Then, the doctor will move
the tip of the catheter

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:44.100
just inside the coronary
artery to be examined.

00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:47.070
A special dye will be
injected into the artery.

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:48.910
This allows your doctor to view it better

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:50.600
with a fluoroscope.

00:01:50.600 --> 00:01:53.653
The dye will make any blockages
in the artery stand out.

00:01:55.200 --> 00:01:57.320
If a significant blockage is found,

00:01:57.320 --> 00:02:00.113
your doctor will insert a
guide wire into the artery.

00:02:02.010 --> 00:02:04.510
A balloon on the tip of
the catheter will be moved

00:02:04.510 --> 00:02:06.393
along the wire to the blockage.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.460
When the balloon inflates,

00:02:09.460 --> 00:02:13.250
it will expand the artery
and improve the blood flow.

00:02:13.250 --> 00:02:16.293
You may feel some chest discomfort
while this is happening.

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:21.123
After this, your doctor will
deflate and remove the balloon.

00:02:25.410 --> 00:02:28.490
A wire mesh tube called
a stent may be placed

00:02:28.490 --> 00:02:30.090
in the treated area.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:33.540
The stent helps keep the
coronary artery open.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.110
Your doctor will choose
the proper size stent,

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:38.073
which is compressed over a balloon.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.100
The stent will be moved into the artery

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.870
over the same guide wire.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:45.430
When the balloon is inflated,

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.313
the stent will expand and lock into place.

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.610
After the balloon catheter is taken out,

00:02:52.610 --> 00:02:55.663
the stent will stay in place
to hold the artery open.

00:02:57.300 --> 00:02:58.520
At the end of the procedure,

00:02:58.520 --> 00:03:00.153
the guide wire will be removed.

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:05.010
To find out more about
coronary artery angioplasty,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:06.693
talk to your healthcare provider.

